From 92e47a8f2be31293d4a7e869198a2164ba35f1db Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: xDarkOne <20536183+xDarkOne@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 22 Oct 2026 16:36:18 +0300
Subject: [PATCH] nvkms: allow HDR on HDMI 3D-capable sinks with shared
 infoframes

nvDpyIsHDRCapable() refuses HDR for every HDMI sink whose EDID
advertises HDMI 3D, because on older display HALs the HDR (DRM)
infoframe and the HDMI VSI share a single vendor-specific slot.

Display HALs with supportsGenericSharedInfoFrames (Blackwell) send
the two infoframes in separate slots, and SendHDRInfoFrame() already
only skips the HDR infoframe for 3D sinks when that capability is
missing. nvDpyIsHDRCapable() was not updated to match, so on these
GPUs HDR is still rejected for any 3D-capable sink: an atomic commit
setting Colorspace to BT2020_RGB/BT2020_YCC fails with -EINVAL from
the driver check, at any resolution and refresh rate, with no log.

Almost every home-cinema projector advertises HDMI 3D, so this makes
HDR unavailable on projectors under Linux while the same projector,
GPU and cable work in HDR on Windows.

Apply the same condition as SendHDRInfoFrame(): only treat HDMI 3D
as incompatible with HDR when the HAL lacks generic shared
infoframes.

Tested on RTX 5090 (GB202), 615.78.08, kernel 7.2.10 (CachyOS),
KWin 6.8.0, Hisense/Vidda C2 Ultra projector over HDMI (EDID: HDMI VSDB
3D_present=1, HDR static metadata with ST2084): before, HDR could not
be enabled at 4K60, 4K30 or 1080p60; after, HDR and BT.2020 enable at
4K60 and the projector switches to HDR.
---
 src/nvidia-modeset/src/nvkms-dpy.c | 7 +++++--
 1 file changed, 5 insertions(+), 2 deletions(-)

diff --git a/src/nvidia-modeset/src/nvkms-dpy.c b/src/nvidia-modeset/src/nvkms-dpy.c
index 3815fe8..80c19b3 100644
--- a/src/nvidia-modeset/src/nvkms-dpy.c
+++ b/src/nvidia-modeset/src/nvkms-dpy.c
@@ -3921,9 +3921,12 @@ NvBool nvDpyIsHDRCapable(const NVDpyEvoRec *pDpyEvo)
 
     /*
      * XXX HDR is not supported with HDMI 3D due to both using VSI
-     * infoframes.
+     * infoframes. This only applies when the HAL cannot send generic
+     * shared infoframes; otherwise the HDR and VSI infoframes use separate
+     * slots (see SendHDRInfoFrame()).
      */
-    if (pInfo->HDMI3DSupported) {
+    if (pInfo->HDMI3DSupported &&
+        !pDpyEvo->pDispEvo->pDevEvo->caps.supportsGenericSharedInfoFrames) {
         return FALSE;
     }
 
